  "query_preview": "..\n(Friday through Tuesday)\nIssued at 1209 AM CDT Wed Oct 7 2026\n\nThe overall upper-level pattern for Friday continues to see the\nridge hold strong across the Western United States, which will\nresult in another dry and warm day. However as move into the\nweekend, model agreement does start to see the upper-level ridge\nbegin to break down with the introduction of both a new trough and\nremnant tropical systems pushing into the West Coast. For the\nPanhandles, these systems will lead to a more zonal to\nsouthwesterly flow for the weekend that will see some slightly\nwarmer temperatures Sunday and Monday. Beyond Monday afternoon,\nmodel agreement is still trending to a full pattern shift as the\ntrough continues to propagate eastward. Should the trend continue\nthen it possible that the Panhandles will be seeing another round\nof active weather starting late Monday. What could be interesting,\nhowever, is that models have been seeing yet another tropical\nsystem getting push up through the Gulf of California and ingest\ninto a secondary trough looking to push in during the middle of\nnext week. The ingestion of this tropical system would lead to\nhigh amounts of moisture over the Panhandles resulting much higher\nthan expected rainfall amounts. However, models have been seeing a\nlot of uncertainty on this tropical system even coming to be, let\nalone it actually making it to our area. For now, present NBM has\ngrown more confident in the two systems bring us activity with\nchance of precipitation around 20 to 40 percent for Tuesday and\nWednesday. Otherwise, look for temperatures to cool with passage\nof these systems with afternoon highs lowing in the low to mid\n70s.",
